**Corollary 27.2 (isolated inverses do not compose at their maximum cutoff).** Even with exactly five retries and total returns supplied, degree three is insufficient for joint raw-history recovery; on that original sublanguage its length-promised threshold is at least four whenever $L\ge25$. This obstructs using the maximum of the isolated retry and return thresholds as a joint inverse.

Proof. In the retry alphabet itself, $XY^3X$ and $YXYXY$ have $c_{XXY}=0,1$ and $c_{XYY}=3,1$, respectively. Their separate $G_3$ therefore differs. The bounded-block supplier in Theorem 15.1 reconstructs every retry word of length at most five at degree three, since its minority count is at most two; the usual $XYYX/YXXY$ collision at degree two gives the sharp generic value $b(5)=3$. Theorem 26.2 likewise gives three for every fixed-retry return family. Yet Theorem 27.1's two five-retry histories have equal whole raw $G_3$, boundary and total returns, and unequal retry and return targets. In particular the whole product does not supply the separate prefix signature that either isolated reconstruction would consume. The source-specific collision transfers to every larger length promise and persists with full original future side information. It does not contradict $r_\mu(L)\le b(L)$, prove a new asymptotic rate, or establish an unbounded return-only threshold with a fixed retry word. $\square$

